      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(4u - 5v = 23\\)\n2. \\(2u + 4v = -8\\)\n\nwe can use the method of elimination or substitution. Here, I'll use the elimination method.\n\nFirst, we'll eliminate one of the variables. We can start by eliminating \\(u\\). To do this, we need to make the coefficients of \\(u\\) in both equations the same. We can achieve this by multiplying the second equation by 2:\n\n\\[2(2u + 4v) = 2(-8)\\]\n\\[4u + 8v = -16\\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n\\[4u - 5v = 23\\]\n\\[4u + 8v = -16\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\(u\\):\n\n\\[(4u + 8v) - (4u - 5v) = -16 - 23\\]\n\\[4u + 8v - 4u + 5v = -39\\]\n\\[13v = -39\\]\n\nSolving for \\(v\\):\n\n\\[v = \\frac{-39}{13}\\]\n\\[v = -3\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(v = -3\\), we can substitute this value back into one of the original equations to solve for \\(u\\). Let's use the second original equation:\n\n\\[2u + 4v = -8\\]\n\\[2u + 4(-3) = -8\\]\n\\[2u - 12 = -8\\]\n\\[2u = -8 + 12\\]\n\\[2u = 4\\]\n\\[u = \\frac{4}{2}\\]\n\\[u = 2\\]\n\nSo, we have \\(u = 2\\) and \\(v = -3\\). The value of \\(u + v\\) is:\n\n\\[u + v = 2 + (-3) = -1\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(u + v\\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
